{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": " Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a medieval prince and military leader who held the position of count from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was a member of the House of Poitiers, a cadet branch of the Angevins.\n\n*Early Life and Ascent to Power*\nRaymond III was born in 1135 as the son of Count Raymond II of Tripoli and his wife, Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early years were shaped by the turbulent political landscape of the Crusader states, particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Upon his father's death in 1152, Raymond III assumed the role of count, beginning his reign under the guidance of older relatives. He immediately faced the challenges of maintaining the county's autonomy and stability amidst the broader regional conflicts.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomatic Efforts*\nOne of Raymond III's most significant contributions was his military leadership during the Crusades. He participated in the Second Crusade and later led campaigns against the Saracens, playing a crucial role in the defense of the Crusader states. His strategic decisions and battlefield prowess were instrumental in securing key victories. Additionally, Raymond III engaged in diplomatic efforts to foster alliances with neighboring rulers, aiming to strengthen the political and military position of Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\nRaymond III's reign left a lasting impact on the history of the Crusader states. He is remembered as a capable leader who effectively managed the county's affairs during a period of intense conflict. His death in 1187, following the Battle of Hattin, marked a significant turning point in the Crusades. Although he did not survive to see the full consequences of the battle, his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states were pivotal. Posthumously, Raymond III's legacy continued to influence the region, and he is celebrated as a key figu